Instagram use is correlated with a 32% increase in body image dissatisfaction among teenage girls, according to Facebook's own internal research leaked in 2021.
Teen girls' rates of anxiety, depression, and self-harm began rising sharply in the US around 2012 — the year smartphone adoption crossed 50% and Instagram launched; the correlation is consistent across 11 countries (Jonathan Haidt, NYU).
A 2018 RCT (Allcott et al., Stanford) found deactivating Facebook for 4 weeks reduced anxiety, increased time with friends and family, and improved subjective wellbeing — demonstrating a causal, not merely correlational, relationship.
